1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,000
A read-only workflow helps maintainers collect evidence, find related reports
and prepare a reply without guessing the cause.

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:33,000
The Dify SDK plugin exposes two read-only tools. A token is optional for
public API access and is configured as a provider secret.

3
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Repository syntax is validated before an API call. Requests use a fixed HTTPS
host and explicit timeouts, with distinct actionable error codes.

4
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:18,000
The tool adapter returns status and payload_json as explicit SDK variables.
The Workflow parses the payload and routes expected errors instead of
inventing a diagnosis.

5
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:26,013
Now the published Web App runs the entire Workflow against a real public
GitHub issue. Captured screens show the real input, execution and result; this
walkthrough is edited for presentation.

6
00:01:26,013 --> 00:01:43,013
The Studio trace shows seven successful nodes in 2.759 seconds, with zero
model tokens. Both API tools and the processing and conditional nodes
executed.

7
00:01:43,013 --> 00:02:03,013
The result identifies the source issue, reports a rule-based connectivity
signal and proposes an investigation order. These are triage signals, not a
confirmed root cause.

8
00:02:03,013 --> 00:02:24,013
Related reports are keyword candidates only. The reply remains a draft for
review, and the workflow never posts it or changes the issue.

9
00:02:24,013 --> 00:02:27,212
An invalid repository is handled by the stop branch. The result explains the
required input format and explicitly says that no diagnosis was generated.

10
00:02:27,212 --> 00:02:47,212
The source, exported DSL and tested Web App are available. This captioned
recording template has no candidate narration; add your own spoken English
explanation before submitting it as the candidate walkthrough.
